{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-7074c67b-280d-4173-9e6e-e03a20a8bf37","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-881df448a509e429","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-a863mpsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Investigation: Default Vector Store Scoping Model (per-namespace vs per-instance)\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the strategy's open question on the scoping model for the default vector store: should the platform provide one pgvector-backed vector store per namespace, one per OGX (Llama Stack) instance, or both? The outcome determines how the provisioning and wiring work is shaped: what gets created, how many PostgreSQL/pgvector instances exist, and how the gen-ai BFF maps vector store connections to the OGXServer instances it creates for playgrounds. The strategy records a preference for per-namespace isolation (noted by the Gen AI Studio lead) but leaves the question open; the per-instance driver originally came from Auto Rag, which has been decoupled from this strategy.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze the OGX vector_io API's namespace semantics (vector store namespaces are tracked in OGX's key-value storage) to determine whether per-namespace isolation is achievable with the remote pgvector provider and what isolation guarantees it provides in multi-tenant clusters.\n- Determine the mapping between namespaces, OGXServer instances, and pgvector databases/roles that the default provisioning should implement, including credential isolation between tenants.\n- Confirm whether any residual requirement (after Auto Rag decoupling) demands per-instance scoping, and if so, define when each model applies.\n- Produce a short decision record stating the adopted scoping model, the isolation guarantees it provides, and the implications for provisioning granularity and BFF wiring.\n\nOut of scope: the provisioning mechanism itself (operator-based vs BFF-managed), which is decided by a separate go/no-go decision; and any Auto Rag scoping requirements, which move to RHAISTRAT-7824.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A decision record exists that selects one scoping model (per-namespace, per-instance, or a defined combination) with the technical rationale grounded in the OGX vector_io namespace behavior.\n- [ ] The decision record states the multi-tenant isolation guarantees of the selected model (credential and data isolation between namespaces).\n- [ ] The decision record states what the selected model implies for provisioning granularity (what Kubernetes resources exist per namespace vs per OGXServer instance) so the provisioning and wiring work can be scoped from it.\n- [ ] Stakeholder sign-off from the Gen AI Studio team on the selected model is recorded.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (AC-5): The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation). This investigation resolves the scoping model that the downstream provisioning and wiring work implements.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The Gen AI Studio lead prefers per-namespace isolation; Auto Rag's possible per-instance need is no longer in this strategy's scope. If per-instance scoping is nonetheless required for a residual case, the provisioning work is rewritten (more instances, different lifecycle) rather than removed.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":75041,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a525bd1676108484","agent_id":"a771cfebd10ea8693","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"32406cfd-b3e0-464f-909f-2c29990d6bc8","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:11:41.518Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
