{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9b8e7efe-737b-48ca-bceb-ad621f67180f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bd654db55778154","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-a863mpsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E004.md","content":"# Wire Gen AI Studio Playground OGXServer Instances to the pgvector vector_io Provider\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic updates the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF so that the OGXServer instances it creates for Gen AI Studio playgrounds with RAG are configured to use the remote pgvector vector_io provider backed by the default PostgreSQL instance, replacing the dependency on the inline Milvus provider that RHAISTRAT-7474 removes from the Llama Stack distribution. The BFF already creates OGXServer instances and hardcodes their storage configuration; this epic extends that code path to enable the pgvector provider and connect it to the provisioned database, following the adopted vector store scoping model.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Extend the gen-ai BFF's OGXServer creation logic (the code path that currently configures storage defaults) to enable the pgvector vector_io provider on playground OGXServer instances and supply the PostgreSQL connection details (endpoint and credentials from the provisioned instance's secret).\n- Implement the connection mapping per the adopted scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance): which provisioned database each playground's OGXServer points at, resolved from the vector store scoping decision.\n- Keep the OGX application storage configuration untouched (SQLite default) — only the vector_io provider configuration changes; vector storage and application storage are independent configurations.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string instead of a self-provisioned instance; the connection source must be a single, replaceable configuration point.\n- Document the migration path for users who outgrow the default store: the OGXServer CR spec accepts user-provided PostgreSQL/vector database connection details, giving a path from the default pgvector instance to a production-grade external vector database aligned with commonly-used providers (resolves the strategy's migration-path open question — it does not change what is built, so it is captured here rather than as a separate epic).\n- Add BFF tests covering the OGXServer vector_io configuration for both the default (provisioned) and user-provided connection cases.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A playground created with RAG capabilities gets an OGXServer instance configured with the pgvector remote vector_io provider connected to the default PostgreSQL instance, with no manual user setup steps.\n- [ ] Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows (document upload, embedding, retrieval) function end-to-end against the default vector store with no loss of current capability compared to the inline Milvus provider.\n- [ ] The connection mapping follows the adopted vector store scoping model and preserves its multi-tenant isolation guarantees (no cross-namespace vector data access).\n- [ ] The OGXServer application storage configuration is unchanged from its current default.\n- [ ] The PostgreSQL connection source is a single replaceable configuration point, demonstrated by a test or configuration switch showing how the shared platform connection string (RHOAI 3.6) replaces the self-provisioned instance without restructuring the BFF.\n- [ ] The documented migration path shows how a user points an OGXServer instance at an external, production-grade vector database via the CR spec connection details.\n- [ ] BFF unit/integration tests cover the vector_io provider wiring for default and user-provided connections, and the build pipeline is green.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (AC-1, partial): Default vector store available out-of-the-box — the BFF wiring makes playground RAG work without manual configuration once the database is provisioned.\n- HLR-2 (AC-2): Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability.\n- HLR-4 (AC-4, partial): The vector store is used as a separate process via the remote provider configuration.\n- HLR-5 (AC-5, partial): Multi-tenant scoping per the adopted model.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This epic is gated by the vector store scoping investigation: if the adopted model differs from per-namespace, the connection-mapping implementation is rewritten (different granularity), not removed.\n- The end-to-end acceptance criteria require a provisioned PostgreSQL instance; the provisioning mechanism (operator-based or BFF-managed, selected by the go/no-go decision) is a separate unit of work — functional validation against any provisioned instance satisfies these criteria.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":76784,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a525bd1676108484","agent_id":"a771cfebd10ea8693","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"f966c7a6-9e59-41cf-9efc-468daf79acf8","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:12:16.655Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
