{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9b8e7efe-737b-48ca-bceb-ad621f67180f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-995d6c3ec8a8468a","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-a863mpsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E005.md","content":"# Benchmark pgvector Against the Inline Milvus Baseline and Set Resource Sizing\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic produces the sprint 1 benchmark and resource sizing for the default pgvector vector store. Per the staff engineer's direction, the default instance is meant for development experimentation, not production usage: strict latency SLAs are not required. The benchmark captures the inline Milvus provider's baseline and verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude for typical playground RAG usage (small document sets, flat scan with no index), and derives the PostgreSQL resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's values.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline for representative playground RAG operations (document ingestion with embedding, similarity query) at typical small-document-set scale.\n- Run the same workload against the remote pgvector provider backed by a `postgresql-16` instance and compare: verify order-of-magnitude parity, not a specific threshold. Flat scan (no vector index) is the expected default; index-type selection and corpus-size-range questions are explicitly out of scope per staff direction.\n- Record resource consumption (CPU, memory) of the pgvector PostgreSQL instance under the benchmark workload and derive recommended requests/limits, starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource values and adjusting based on results.\n- Publish a short benchmark report with the comparison, the sizing recommendation, and the guidance that users with production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A benchmark report exists that captures the inline Milvus baseline and the pgvector results for representative playground RAG workloads.\n- [ ] The report concludes whether pgvector is within the same order of magnitude of the inline Milvus baseline for typical playground usage, with the workload definition and measurements attached.\n- [ ] A resource sizing recommendation (requests/limits) for the default pgvector PostgreSQL instance is published, derived from the model-registry-operator's reference values and the benchmark data.\n- [ ] The report states the dev-experimentation positioning: no latency SLAs are claimed, and production-scale workloads are directed to an external vector database.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-6 (AC-6): The default vector store is lightweight enough to run in resource-constrained environments without imposing significant overhead — the sizing recommendation is the deliverable that makes this requirement testable.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- If the benchmark shows pgvector is not in the same order of magnitude for typical usage, the outcome is fix-and-retry (sizing adjustment, workload investigation) — it does not change which epics exist, which is why this is a benchmark epic with a defined pass/fail criterion rather than a gating investigation.\n- Latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99), corpus size ranges, and index type selection were explicitly removed from scope by the staff engineer.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":76784,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a525bd1676108484","agent_id":"a771cfebd10ea8693","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"a3196b81-e948-4e9c-b7f3-920fe84b4ea6","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:12:24.783Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
