{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-3b741910-8920-4268-8745-17407a88be5c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86c46005c1147675","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-a863mpsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006.md","content":"# BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n\n## Description\n\nThis conditional-branch epic implements BFF-managed provisioning of the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio, as the fallback if the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5. The gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, following the pattern the BFF already uses to provision guardrails infrastructure.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement BFF-managed creation of the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (from the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image, which includes pgvector) with the resource requests/limits recommended by the benchmark, per the adopted vector store scoping model, when a playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists in the namespace.\n- Design the lifecycle handling explicitly, since the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, recovery after BFF pod restarts (reconciling expected vs. existing resources on demand), and credential secret management.\n- Wire the image reference for disconnected installs by reusing the `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` environment variable already present in the rhods-operator CSV — no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding, and no new RELATED_IMAGE entry unless the existing variable cannot be shared.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC arrives (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string instead of self-provisioning; keep the connection source replaceable so the provisioning code path can be retired without restructuring the OGXServer wiring.\n- Add BFF tests covering provisioning, idempotent re-creation checks, cleanup on deletion, and restart recovery.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A playground with RAG configured in a namespace gets a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance provisioned automatically by the BFF (no manual user setup), running as a separate process from the OGX server.\n- [ ] The provisioning follows the adopted vector store scoping model and preserves its multi-tenant isolation guarantees.\n- [ ] The PostgreSQL instance uses the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image (pgvector included) with resource requests/limits per the benchmark recommendation, and the image reference resolves in disconnected installs via the RELATED_IMAGE mechanism.\n- [ ] Lifecycle behavior is implemented and tested: playground deletion cleans up the instance; BFF pod restarts do not orphan or duplicate instances; credentials are managed via secrets.\n- [ ] The connection consumed by the playground's OGXServer wiring comes from a single replaceable configuration point, swappable to the RHOAI 3.6 shared platform connection string.\n- [ ] BFF tests cover provisioning, idempotency, cleanup, and restart recovery, and the build pipeline is green.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (AC-1): A default vector store is available out-of-the-box without users manually provisioning or configuring external infrastructure.\n- HLR-4 (AC-4, partial): The provisioned database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance.\n- HLR-5 (AC-5, partial): Provisioning follows the adopted multi-tenant scoping model.\n- HLR-6 (AC-6, partial): Sizing per the benchmark recommendation keeps the instance lightweight.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This branch exists only if the go/no-go decision selects the BFF-managed fallback (RHAISTRAT-7164 not landing in time for RHOAI 3.5). If that outcome does not hold, this epic is removed in favor of the operator-based provisioning branch. If BFF-managed provisioning is also rejected, the final no-build fallback is requiring users to bring their own PostgreSQL by manually configuring the OGXServer CR spec with their connection details — consistent with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0, though the Playground largely abstracts OGX from users.\n- The BFF is not an operator: cleanup on deletion, pod-restart recovery, and credential rotation need the explicit design attention called out in the scope; these are the main technical risks of this branch.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":79497,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a525bd1676108484","agent_id":"a771cfebd10ea8693","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"67da87c0-a97c-4e0d-8cc9-9105f1371043","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:12:48.570Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
