{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-acc4fd7d6b35454c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n11\t\n12\t## Input Health Warnings\n13\t\n14\t- The strategy body states the full strategy exceeds Jira's size limit and is stored as attachment `RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`. That attachment is not present in the workspace, so the Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table, formal P0/P1/P2 HLR list, Dependencies table, and Non-Functional Requirements could not be parsed. HLRs below are derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria and Success Criteria; owner teams are inferred (see Health Warnings).\n15\t- The **Staff Engineer Input** section is non-empty and takes precedence over the (absent) AI-generated technical approach. All staff corrections and directives are logged under \"Staff Engineer Input Overrides\" below and are reflected in the epic scope.\n16\t\n17\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n18\t\n19\tThe following staff corrections and directives override or reshape the AI-generated strategy content:\n20\t\n21\t1. **OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL** — OGX defaults to SQLite for application storage; the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite when creating OGXServer instances. Introducing PostgreSQL is new work, not an extension of existing infrastructure.\n22\t2. **No custom image build, no Konflux onboarding** — `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` already includes pgvector, and `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is already in the rhods-operator CSV. The strategy's 1-2 sprint image-onboarding estimate is eliminated; no `repo-onboarding` / `konflux-onboarding` / `license-validation` epics are generated.\n23\t3. **Platform-level shared PostgreSQL via DSC is deferred to RHOAI 3.6** — for 3.5, Gen AI Studio must self-provision its PostgreSQL. The self-provisioning implementation must be designed for portability to the shared connection string in 3.6.\n24\t4. **Hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164 for operator-based provisioning** — self-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern requires a RHOAI-controlled operator, which the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164, In Progress) delivers. Uncertain timing → modeled as a go/no-go Investigation with conditional branches.\n25\t5. **Fallbacks if RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land** — BFF-managed provisioning (like the existing guardrails infrastructure provisioning) or bring-your-own PostgreSQL. The dev team defines a go/no-go gate in sprint planning.\n26\t6. **Auto Rag decoupled** — Auto Rag integration targets RHOAI 3.6 via RHAISTRAT-7824. This strategy validates pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG workflows. Auto Rag is removed from affected components, acceptance criteria, and validation (see HLR-3 descoping note).\n27\t7. **Performance thresholds removed** — the default pgvector instance is for development experimentation; no latency SLAs. Sprint 1 benchmark captures the inline Milvus baseline and verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude. Flat scan (no index) is sufficient; index-type and corpus-size open questions are removed.\n28\t8. **Resource sizing** — start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjust after benchmarking.\n29\t9. **Vector storage is independent of application storage** — only the vector_io provider (`remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) is configured; the OGX application storage backend remains SQLite. No SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concerns; no consolidation of vector and application storage.\n30\t10. **Remote vector store implementation is reusable** — the existing remote vector store implementation in OGX can be largely reused for pgvector; the majority of effort is PostgreSQL/pgvector infrastructure and provisioning.\n31\t\n32\t## Epic List\n33\t\n34\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n35\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Investigation: default vector store scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance) | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Investigation: go/no-go on default vector store provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5 | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Enable pgvector remote vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | OGX Core | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Wire Gen AI Studio playground OGXServer instances to the pgvector vector_io provider | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E005 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set resource sizing | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E006 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio | Implementation (branch A) | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio | Implementation (branch B) | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n43\t\n44\tBranch epics are conditional alternatives selected by the outcome of the provisioning go/no-go investigation; they are attached to that investigation as a plan document, not created as standalone Jira issues.\n45\t\n46\t## Dependency DAG\n47\t\n48\t```mermaid\n49\tgraph TD\n50\t    E001[\"E001 Scoping model investigation\"] --> E004[\"E004 BFF wires OGXServer vector_io\"]\n51\t    E002[\"E002 Provisioning go/no-go investigation\"]\n52\t    E003[\"E003 OGX pgvector vector_io provider\"] --> E004\n53\t    E003 --> E005[\"E005 Benchmark + resource sizing\"]\n54\t    E001 -.-> BR_A[\"BRANCH-A-E006 Operator-based provisioning\"]\n55\t    E001 -.-> BR_B[\"BRANCH-B-E006 BFF-managed provisioning\"]\n56\t    E002 -. selects branch .-> BR_A\n57\t    E002 -. selects branch .-> BR_B\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\tSolid arrows are main-plan blocking edges (dependency → dependent). Dashed arrows are conditional-branch gates: each branch epic's frontmatter lists the scoping and go/no-go investigations in `dependencies`, with `gated_by` pointing at the go/no-go investigation.\n61\t\n62\t## DAG Justification\n63\t\n64\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n65\t|------|------|-----------|\n66\t| E001 → E004 | Investigation edge (Rule 3) | The scoping outcome (per-namespace vs per-instance) rewrites how the BFF maps vector store connections to OGXServer instances — a true gate with `rewrite` impact recorded on E004. |\n67\t| E003 → E004 | Implementation → Implementation (Rule 13) | E003 produces the provider capability (pgvector vector_io provider available and configurable in the OGX distribution) that E004's OGXServer configuration builds against. |\n68\t| E003 → E005 | Implementation → Implementation (Rule 13) | The benchmark requires a runnable pgvector vector_io provider to measure against the inline Milvus baseline. |\n69\t| E001 → branches, E002 → branches | Investigation edge (Rule 3, bounded outcomes) | The provisioning approach decision has ≤3 bounded outcomes (operator-based / BFF-managed / bring-your-own). Branch epics are the conditional decomposition; both are gated by the go/no-go investigation (`gated_by`) and blocked by the scoping investigation. |\n70\t| No edge E002 → E003/E004/E005 | Tiered delivery (Step 5.5, Rule 17 analog) | Provider enablement, BFF wiring, and benchmarking are Tier 1 work that delivers partial value (risk reduction and the reusable provider capability) regardless of which provisioning mechanism is selected; they proceed in parallel with the go/no-go decision. |\n71\t| No konflux-onboarding / repo-onboarding / license-validation chain | Onboarding rules N/A (Staff Correction 2) | The Red Hat `postgresql-16` image already ships pgvector and is already referenced in the rhods-operator CSV; no new repo, image, or build pipeline is introduced. |\n72\t| No edge between E001 and E002 | Rule 14 | The scoping question and the provisioning-approach go/no-go are independent unknowns resolved by different deliverables; both start immediately. |\n73\t\n74\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n75\t\n76\tThe strategy's formal HLR table is in the unavailable attachment; HLRs are derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (AC-n below). Priorities follow the strategy's Blocker-level frontmatter priority and the capability criticality stated in the problem statement.\n77\t\n78\t| HLR (derived) | Source | Priority | Epic(s) |\n79\t|---------------|--------|----------|---------|\n80\t| HLR-1: Default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure | AC-1 | P0 | E004, BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 |\n81\t| HLR-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | AC-2 | P0 | E003, E004, E005 |\n82\t| HLR-3: Auto Rag functions with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | AC-3 | P0 | **Descoped** per Staff Engineer Input (Auto Rag compatibility moves to RHAISTRAT-7824, targeting RHOAI 3.6) — not covered by this decomposition |\n83\t| HLR-4: The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not an inline provider) | AC-4 | P0 | E003, E004 |\n84\t| HLR-5: The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation) | AC-5 | P1 | E001, E004, BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 |\n85\t| HLR-6: The default vector store is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead | AC-6 | P1 | E005, BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-B-E006 |\n86\t\n87\tAdditional staff-directed requirements traced: portability to the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL (E004, BRANCH-B-E006 scope), reuse of the model-registry-operator sizing reference (E005), and reuse of `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` for disconnected installs (branch epics). The strategy's migration-path open question (Bram Sundqvist) does not change which epics exist — it is captured as an acceptance criterion on E004 (documented configuration for pointing OGXServer at an external vector database).\n88\t\n89\t## Health Warnings\n90\t\n91\t- **Missing strategy attachment** (non-blocking): the Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR sections were not available; this decomposition is built from the summary, acceptance criteria, open questions, and Staff Engineer Input (which takes precedence). Human review should confirm no scope in the attachment is orphaned.\n92\t- **Team inference** (non-blocking): owner teams were inferred because the Impacted Teams table is unavailable. The gen-ai BFF / playground / benchmark / provisioning work is assigned to the Gen AI Studio team (the strategy is labeled `dashboard-crimson-scrum` and the Gen AI Studio lead is quoted throughout); the ogx-distribution provider work is assigned to the OGX Core team.\n93\t- **Auto Rag descoping** (staff override): AC-3 (Auto Rag) is deliberately not covered — RHAISTRAT-7824 owns Auto Rag compatibility with pgvector in 3.6. Reviewers should confirm the RHAISTRAT-8004 Jira acceptance criteria are updated to match, otherwise the epic set will appear to orphan AC-3.\n94\t- **Schedule risk, not an AC** (scope note): the strategy cannot be committed to a specific release until RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) delivers or the go/no-go gate selects the BFF-managed fallback. This timing is externally controlled and is modeled as the go/no-go investigation, not as an acceptance criterion on any epic.\n95\t- **Scope trap avoided**: the strategy's \"no loss of current capability\" acceptance criterion must be read functionally (RAG workflows work), not as a performance parity SLA — staff explicitly removed latency thresholds; the benchmark epic only verifies order-of-magnitude parity with the inline Milvus baseline.\n96\t- **Priority note**: E005 is P1 (HLR-6, staff-classified dev-experimentation scope with no SLAs) even though the strategy is Blocker-level; it blocks no P0 work.\n97\t\n98\t## Tiered Delivery\n99\t\n100\tThe RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing is the uncertain external dependency:\n101\t\n102\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E001 (scoping decision), E002 (go/no-go gate definition), E003 (pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution — reusable regardless of provisioning mechanism), E004 (BFF OGXServer wiring — needed in every outcome), E005 (benchmark + sizing — risk reduction). Partial value: the pgvector capability is proven and wired before the provisioning mechanism lands.\n103\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: the provisioning branch epics. BRANCH-A-E006 (operator-based, gated on RHAISTRAT-7164 landing for 3.5) or BRANCH-B-E006 (BFF-managed fallback). Bring-your-own PostgreSQL (users manually configure the OGXServer CR spec with their connection details) is the final no-build fallback recorded in BRANCH-B-E006's gate failure impact, aligned with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0.\n104\t\n105\t## Component Mappings\n106\t\n107\tCanonical component names (from `.context/rhai-components.txt`) used by the epics, with mapping notes:\n108\t\n109\t| Strategy surface | Canonical component | Note |\n110\t|------------------|---------------------|------|\n111\t| Gen AI Studio playground, gen-ai BFF (odh-dashboard repo) | `Gen AI Studio` | The strategy's playground/BFF work maps to the Gen AI Studio canonical component; the gen-ai BFF is the Gen AI Studio backend in the odh-dashboard federated architecture. |\n112\t| OGX / Llama Stack distribution (vector_io provider) | `OGX Core` | The ogx-distribution repo's remote vector_io provider work maps to OGX Core. |\n113\t| Auto Rag | (removed) | Removed per Staff Engineer Input; owned by RHAISTRAT-7824. |\n114\t| PostgreSQL/pgvector | (no epic) | Existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image with pgvector; no platform component introduced (Staff Correction 2). |\n115\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fddf87e508decaa","agent_id":"a19263f15963a2495","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"6c7d0027-961d-4d2d-baac-9f686e129d9e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:15:15.644Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-acc4fd7d6b35454c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
