{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7dafe2a466bc908","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E004\n3\ttitle: Wire Gen AI Studio playground OGXServer instances to the pgvector vector_io\n4\t  provider\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E003\n13\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: rewrite\n16\t  fallback_approach: If the adopted scoping model is not per-namespace, rewrite the\n17\t    BFF connection mapping to the selected granularity (e.g. one vector store per\n18\t    OGXServer instance) without changing the provider wiring itself.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 1\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 6\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Wire Gen AI Studio Playground OGXServer Instances to the pgvector vector_io Provider\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tThis epic updates the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF so that the OGXServer instances it creates for Gen AI Studio playgrounds with RAG are configured to use the remote pgvector vector_io provider backed by the default PostgreSQL instance, replacing the dependency on the inline Milvus provider that RHAISTRAT-7474 removes from the Llama Stack distribution. The BFF already creates OGXServer instances and hardcodes their storage configuration; this epic extends that code path to enable the pgvector provider and connect it to the provisioned database, following the adopted vector store scoping model.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Extend the gen-ai BFF's OGXServer creation logic (the code path that currently configures storage defaults) to enable the pgvector vector_io provider on playground OGXServer instances and supply the PostgreSQL connection details (endpoint and credentials from the provisioned instance's secret).\n45\t- Implement the connection mapping per the adopted scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance): which provisioned database each playground's OGXServer points at, resolved from the vector store scoping decision.\n46\t- Keep the OGX application storage configuration untouched (SQLite default) — only the vector_io provider configuration changes; vector storage and application storage are independent configurations.\n47\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string instead of a self-provisioned instance; the connection source must be a single, replaceable configuration point.\n48\t- Document the migration path for users who outgrow the default store: the OGXServer CR spec accepts user-provided PostgreSQL/vector database connection details, giving a path from the default pgvector instance to a production-grade external vector database aligned with commonly-used providers (resolves the strategy's migration-path open question — it does not change what is built, so it is captured here rather than as a separate epic).\n49\t- Add BFF tests covering the OGXServer vector_io configuration for both the default (provisioned) and user-provided connection cases.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- [ ] A playground created with RAG capabilities gets an OGXServer instance configured with the pgvector remote vector_io provider connected to the default PostgreSQL instance, with no manual user setup steps.\n54\t- [ ] Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows (document upload, embedding, retrieval) function end-to-end against the default vector store with no loss of current capability compared to the inline Milvus provider.\n55\t- [ ] The connection mapping follows the adopted vector store scoping model and preserves its multi-tenant isolation guarantees (no cross-namespace vector data access).\n56\t- [ ] The OGXServer application storage configuration is unchanged from its current default.\n57\t- [ ] The PostgreSQL connection source is a single replaceable configuration point, demonstrated by a test or configuration switch showing how the shared platform connection string (RHOAI 3.6) replaces the self-provisioned instance without restructuring the BFF.\n58\t- [ ] The documented migration path shows how a user points an OGXServer instance at an external, production-grade vector database via the CR spec connection details.\n59\t- [ ] BFF unit/integration tests cover the vector_io provider wiring for default and user-provided connections, and the build pipeline is green.\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- HLR-1 (AC-1, partial): Default vector store available out-of-the-box — the BFF wiring makes playground RAG work without manual configuration once the database is provisioned.\n64\t- HLR-2 (AC-2): Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability.\n65\t- HLR-4 (AC-4, partial): The vector store is used as a separate process via the remote provider configuration.\n66\t- HLR-5 (AC-5, partial): Multi-tenant scoping per the adopted model.\n67\t\n68\t## Risks / Notes\n69\t\n70\t- This epic is gated by the vector store scoping investigation: if the adopted model differs from per-namespace, the connection-mapping implementation is rewritten (different granularity), not removed.\n71\t- The end-to-end acceptance criteria require a provisioned PostgreSQL instance; the provisioning mechanism (operator-based or BFF-managed, selected by the go/no-go decision) is a separate unit of work — functional validation against any provisioned instance satisfies these criteria.\n72\t"}]},