rable, not deferrable features, and the genuinely deferrable P1 work (benchmark/sizing) is already a separate P1 epic. Minor: the summary's traceability matrix and the epic HLR Traceability sections disagree in two places — the matrix maps HLR-2 to E005 but E005's own section lists only HLR-6, and both branches list HLR-4 (partial) while the matrix maps HLR-4 only to E003/E004. The formal HLR list was unavailable (missing strategy attachment); the derived matrix plus the health warning is an acceptable handling.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\nNo cycles; all edges reference existing epics; diagram direction (dependency → dependent) matches frontmatter `dependencies` exactly for every solid and dashed edge; branch files set both `branch` and `gated_by`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies`; the konflux/repo-onboarding parallelism rules are vacuous (no such epics, per Staff Correction 2); the E002-does-not-gate-Tier-1 omission is explicitly justified by tiered delivery, and the critical path of 2 reflects deliberate, documented parallelism. However, there is a critical missing edge discoverable from epic content: E005's AC-3 publishes \"a resource sizing recommendation (requests/limits) for the default pgvector PostgreSQL instance,\" and both BRANCH-A-E006 and BRANCH-B-E006 consume it — scope: \"with the resource requests/limits recommended by the benchmark\"; AC: \"resource requests/limits per the benchmark recommendation\" — yet neither branch lists E005 in `dependencies` and the summary DAG (and its justification table, which discusses E002's non-edges but is silent on E005) has no E005-to-branch edge. As written, the branch ACs cannot be verified without E005's deliverable. Note the summary's claim that E005 \"blocks no P0 work\" is falsified by the branches' own AC text. Fix either by adding the edge and raising E005 to P0 (to satisfy priority inheritance), or by decoupling the branch ACs to reference the model-registry-operator values as the sizing baseline with benchmark refinement as adjustment. Minor: E004's end-to-end ACs require a provisioned instance that only a conditional branch produces; no valid DAG edge can express this (main-plan → branch edges are forbidden) and E004's Risks/Notes documents the workaround (\"functional validation against any provisioned instance satisfies these criteria\") — make that precondition explicit in the AC text or move end-to-end validation into the branch ACs.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nComponent/team tuples are clean: OGX Core owns the distribution provider work (E003) while Gen AI Studio owns BFF wiring, benchmark, and provisioning (E001/E002/E004/E005/branches) — separate epics per tuple, matching the canonical component list. No epic exceeds sprint scale. The two branch epics are mutually exclusive conditional alternatives, not scope duplication; E004 (consumer/wiring) and the branches (producer/provisioning) are complementary, and the shared \"single replaceable configuration point\" is an interface contract asserted on both sides rather than duplicated work. Minor: E004 bundles a user-facing documentation deliverable (the documented migration path to an external vector database, with its own AC) with BFF code implementation — content authoring and code are different kinds of work with different review criteria.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's scoping outcome rewrites E004's connection mapping (recorded as a `rewrite` gate with fallback) and the branches' provisioning granularity; E002's go/no-go selects which branch exists (`remove` gates with fallbacks on both branches). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct `dependencies`; every gated epic has `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated. E005 is correctly typed as Implementation (benchmark report + sizing recommendation as deliverables) with a sound explanation of why it is not a gating investigation. Minor: the branch epics list two Investigations (E001, E002) but the scalar `gated_by` records only the E002 existence gate; E001's scope-rewrite impact on the branches is recorded only on E004's gate_failure_impact, not on the branches' — the choice of the existence-determining gate is the right one, but the E001 impact on the branches is undocumented gate metadata.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), all five Implementation epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-type contamination. All seven ai-signals rationale files exist and their values match the frontmatter. Signal values are consistent with strategy content: E002's `source_accessibility: 0` correctly reflects that the answer is pending delivery status (the rubric's error case would be marking it 1), `human_judgment_required: -2` matches the staff-directed sprint-planning gate; E004's `open_questions: -1` matches the unresolved scoping model; Branch A's `existing_foundation: 0` / `external_dependency: -1` match the not-yet-delivered RHAISTRAT-7164 operator layer; Branch B's `existing_foundation: 1` / `open_questions: -1` match the existing guardrails pattern with the lifecycle design question. Rationales are substantive, citing specific files and architecture context.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated AC handling: no konflux-chain or docs-authoring epics exist, so those rules are vacuous; for the provider replacement, E003 carries the config-only activation AC (environment guard, no code changes to switch from the inline provider), which is the feature-flag mechanism. E002 correctly models the externally controlled RHAISTRAT-7164 timing as a schedule risk in Risks/Notes rather than misfiling it as an AC — exactly the soft-AC pattern to avoid. 