{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f0b79ba0699a546","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing DAG edge: BRANCH-A-E006 and BRANCH-B-E006 both consume E005''s\n10\t    published resource-sizing recommendation (scope: resource requests/limits recommended\n11\t    by the benchmark; AC: resource requests/limits per the benchmark recommendation)\n12\t    but neither branch lists E005 in dependencies and the summary DAG has no E005-to-branch\n13\t    edge. E005 AC-3 explicitly publishes this artifact, so the branch ACs cannot be\n14\t    verified without it; the summary''s claim that E005 blocks no P0 work is falsified\n15\t    by the branch AC text. Fix by adding the edge and raising E005 to P0 (to satisfy\n16\t    priority inheritance), or by decoupling the branch ACs to reference the model-registry-operator\n17\t    values as the sizing baseline with benchmark refinement as adjustment.'\n18\t- severity: minor\n19\t  criterion: HLR Coverage\n20\t  description: 'HLR traceability matrix and epic HLR Traceability sections disagree:\n21\t    the matrix maps HLR-2 to E005 but E005''s own section lists only HLR-6, and both\n22\t    branch epics list HLR-4 (partial) while the matrix maps HLR-4 only to E003 and\n23\t    E004.'\n24\t- severity: minor\n25\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n26\t  description: E004's end-to-end ACs require a provisioned PostgreSQL instance that\n27\t    only a conditional-branch epic produces; no valid DAG edge can express this (main-plan-to-branch\n28\t    edges are forbidden) and the workaround is only documented in E004's Risks/Notes\n29\t    — make the validation precondition explicit in the AC text or move end-to-end\n30\t    validation into the branch ACs.\n31\t- severity: minor\n32\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n33\t  description: E004 bundles a user-facing documentation deliverable (the documented\n34\t    migration path to an external vector database, with its own AC) with BFF code\n35\t    implementation; content authoring and code are different kinds of work and should\n36\t    be separated.\n37\t- severity: minor\n38\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n39\t  description: Branch epics list two Investigations (E001, E002) in dependencies but\n40\t    the scalar gated_by records only the E002 existence gate; E001's scope-rewrite\n41\t    impact on the branches is recorded only on E004's gate_failure_impact, not on\n42\t    the branches' gate metadata.\n43\t- severity: minor\n44\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n45\t  description: E004 replaces the BFF's inline-Milvus provider wiring but has no explicit\n46\t    rollback/feature-flag AC; the config-only activation toggle exists only on E003\n47\t    at the distribution level.\n48\terror: null\n49\t---\n50\t## Review Summary\n51\t\n52\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n53\tRecommendation: revise\n54\t\n55\tcontract validator: OK (skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8004 exited 0). Triage check: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 7-epic multi-branch output is expected and not penalized.\n56\t\n57\tThe decomposition is strong overall — staff engineer input is faithfully applied (Auto Rag descoping, no Konflux/image-onboarding chain, SQLite/pgvector separation, tiered delivery around the RHAISTRAT-7164 uncertainty), branch files carry both `branch` and `gated_by`, and no main-plan epic depends on a branch node. It fails on one structural defect: both conditional-branch epics consume an artifact produced by E005 (the published resource-sizing recommendation) in their scope and acceptance criteria, but the DAG has no edge from E005 to either branch. Per the missing-edge rule this zeroes Criterion 2 and forces `pass: false`.\n58\t\n59\t## Criterion Details\n60\t\n61\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n62\tAll six derived HLRs are covered or explicitly descoped with justification: HLR-1 (E004 + both branches), HLR-2 (E003, E004), HLR-4 (E003, E004), HLR-5 (E001, E004, branches), HLR-6 (E005, branches). HLR-3 (Auto Rag) is descoped per explicit Staff Engineer Input (moves to RHAISTRAT-7824) and flagged in Health Warnings — a justified, staff-directed descope, not a gap. Priority inheritance is correct: E001, E002, E003 (the epics blocking P0 work) are all P0; E005 is P1 and blocks nothing in the declared DAG. No priority collapse: the P1 HLRs bundled into P0 epics (HLR-5/6 in the branches, HLR-5 in E004) are incidental to the P0 deliverable, not deferrable features, and the genuinely deferrable P1 work (benchmark/sizing) is already a separate P1 epic. Minor: the summary's traceability matrix and the epic HLR Traceability sections disagree in two places — the matrix maps HLR-2 to E005 but E005's own section lists only HLR-6, and both branches list HLR-4 (partial) while the matrix maps HLR-4 only to E003/E004. The formal HLR list was unavailable (missing strategy attachment); the derived matrix plus the health warning is an acceptable handling.\n63\t\n64\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n65\tNo cycles; all edges reference existing epics; diagram direction (dependency → dependent) matches frontmatter `dependencies` exactly for every solid and dashed edge; branch files set both `branch` and `gated_by`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies`; the konflux/repo-onboarding parallelism rules are vacuous (no such epics, per Staff Correction 2); the E002-does-not-gate-Tier-1 omission is explicitly justified by tiered delivery, and the critical path of 2 reflects deliberate, documented parallelism. However, there is a critical missing edge discoverable from epic content: E005's AC-3 publishes \"a resource sizing recommendation (requests/limits) for the default pgvector PostgreSQL instance,\" and both BRANCH-A-E006 and BRANCH-B-E006 consume it — scope: \"with the resource requests/limits recommended by the benchmark\"; AC: \"resource requests/limits per the benchmark recommendation\" — yet neither branch lists E005 in `dependencies` and the summary DAG (and its justification table, which discusses E002's non-edges but is silent on E005) has no E005-to-branch edge. As written, the branch ACs cannot be verified without E005's deliverable. Note the summary's claim that E005 \"blocks no P0 work\" is falsified by the branches' own AC text. Fix either by adding the edge and raising E005 to P0 (to satisfy priority inheritance), or by decoupling the branch ACs to reference the model-registry-operator values as the sizing baseline with benchmark refinement as adjustment. Minor: E004's end-to-end ACs require a provisioned instance that only a conditional branch produces; no valid DAG edge can express this (main-plan → branch edges are forbidden) and E004's Risks/Notes documents the workaround (\"functional validation against any provisioned instance satisfies these criteria\") — make that precondition explicit in the AC text or move end-to-end validation into the branch ACs.\n66\t\n67\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n68\tComponent/team tuples are clean: OGX Core owns the distribution provider work (E003) while Gen AI Studio owns BFF wiring, benchmark, and provisioning (E001/E002/E004/E005/branches) — separate epics per tuple, matching the canonical component list. No epic exceeds sprint scale. The two branch epics are mutually exclusive conditional alternatives, not scope duplication; E004 (consumer/wiring) and the branches (producer/provisioning) are complementary, and the shared \"single replaceable configuration point\" is an interface contract asserted on both sides rather than duplicated work. Minor: E004 bundles a user-facing documentation deliverable (the documented migration path to an external vector database, with its own AC) with BFF code implementation — content authoring and code are different kinds of work with different review criteria.\n69\t\n70\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n71\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's scoping outcome rewrites E004's connection mapping (recorded as a `rewrite` gate with fallback) and the branches' provisioning granularity; E002's go/no-go selects which branch exists (`remove` gates with fallbacks on both branches). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct `dependencies`; every gated epic has `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated. E005 is correctly typed as Implementation (benchmark report + sizing recommendation as deliverables) with a sound explanation of why it is not a gating investigation. Minor: the branch epics list two Investigations (E001, E002) but the scalar `gated_by` records only the E002 existence gate; E001's scope-rewrite impact on the branches is recorded only on E004's gate_failure_impact, not on the branches' — the choice of the existence-determining gate is the right one, but the E001 impact on the branches is undocumented gate metadata.\n72\t\n73\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n74\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), all five Implementation epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-type contamination. All seven ai-signals rationale files exist and their values match the frontmatter. Signal values are consistent with strategy content: E002's `source_accessibility: 0` correctly reflects that the answer is pending delivery status (the rubric's error case would be marking it 1), `human_judgment_required: -2` matches the staff-directed sprint-planning gate; E004's `open_questions: -1` matches the unresolved scoping model; Branch A's `existing_foundation: 0` / `external_dependency: -1` match the not-yet-delivered RHAISTRAT-7164 operator layer; Branch B's `existing_foundation: 1` / `open_questions: -1` match the existing guardrails pattern with the lifecycle design question. Rationales are substantive, citing specific files and architecture context.\n75\t\n76\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n77\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated AC handling: no konflux-chain or docs-authoring epics exist, so those rules are vacuous; for the provider replacement, E003 carries the config-only activation AC (environment guard, no code changes to switch from the inline provider), which is the feature-flag mechanism. E002 correctly models the externally controlled RHAISTRAT-7164 timing as a schedule risk in Risks/Notes rather than misfiling it as an AC — exactly the soft-AC pattern to avoid. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by scan; Jira keys like RHAISTRAT-7474 are legitimate references). Minor: E004, which replaces the BFF's inline-Milvus wiring (the user-visible replacement), has no explicit rollback/feature-flag AC of its own — the toggle lives only on E003 at the distribution level.\n78\t\n79\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n80\tAll strategy acceptance criteria are accounted for: AC-1 through AC-6 map to the epic set (with AC-3 descoped per staff direction and explicitly flagged for Jira AC updates). Both strategy open questions are resolved into the plan (scoping model → E001; migration path → E004 AC). All staff directives are traced: portability to the 3.6 shared database (E004 + branches), model-registry sizing reference (E005), RELATED_IMAGE reuse for disconnected installs (branches), vector/application storage separation (E003, E004), reusable remote implementation (E003), performance descoping (E005), and the fallback ladder (operator-based → BFF-managed → bring-your-own, with the no-build outcome recorded in the branches' gate_failure_impact and E004's user-provided-connection AC delivering the capability it needs). Conditional branches cover all three bounded outcomes of the go/no-go. Strategy-level risks and the missing-attachment caveat are carried forward in Health Warnings and epic Risks/Notes rather than silently dropped.\n81\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8764aeab3391d186","agent_id":"a4e9a9654cdcf4b49","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"4cff32b0-67c3-4fc1-ad50-fb4bd23ac1f9","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:20:33.706Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f0b79ba0699a546","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
