{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-a1c84f88-2d62-410c-b3db-33235a2b7a09","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-af5662cf89963f36","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-a863mpsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E007.md","content":"# Document the Migration Path from the Default Vector Store to an External Vector Database\n\n## Description\n\nThis docs-authoring epic produces the user-facing documentation for migrating off the default pgvector-backed vector store: how a user who outgrows the development-experimentation default points their Gen AI Studio playground's OGXServer instance at an external, production-grade vector database via user-provided connection details in the OGXServer CR spec. It resolves the strategy's migration-path open question (aligning with commonly-used vector database providers for easier migration) as a documentation deliverable that follows the implementation. Content authoring and code are different kinds of work with different review criteria, so this deliverable is separated from the BFF wiring implementation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Write the user-facing migration documentation: when to move off the default pgvector instance (production-scale vector workloads), and how to configure an OGXServer instance with user-provided PostgreSQL/vector database connection details via the CR spec, aligned with commonly-used vector database providers.\n- Document the constraints carried over from the implementation: the default instance is positioned for development experimentation (no latency SLAs), the migration is a configuration change at the single replaceable connection point, and the OGX application storage backend is unaffected.\n- Place the documentation in the existing Gen AI Studio user documentation set, following its structure and style conventions.\n- Technically review the content against the delivered implementation before publication.\n\nOut of scope: any code changes to the BFF or the OGX distribution; the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC (RHOAI 3.6) consumption story.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Published documentation walks a user through pointing an OGXServer instance at an external, production-grade vector database via the CR spec connection details, including credential handling, with no manual re-wiring of the playground.\n- [ ] The documentation states the positioning of the default store (development experimentation) and the trigger for migrating (production-scale vector workloads).\n- [ ] The documentation is technically reviewed against the delivered implementation (the OGXServer CR spec connection surface and the replaceable connection point as built) and the review sign-off is recorded; any discrepancies found in review are fed back and resolved.\n- [ ] The documentation follows the existing Gen AI Studio documentation structure and style, and builds cleanly in the docs pipeline.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- No HLR is directly satisfied by this documentation deliverable. It resolves the strategy's migration-path open question (recording the path from the default store that satisfies HLR-1 to a production-grade external vector database), complementing the out-of-the-box default rather than implementing it.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This epic must not be started before the implementation it documents is delivered: the BFF wiring implementation (user-provided connection support and the single replaceable connection point), the OGX distribution provider enablement, and the benchmark/sizing positioning all feed the content. It is blocked on all main-plan implementation work per the docs-last rule.\n- If the OGXServer CR spec connection surface changes after RHOAI 3.5 (e.g., the RHOAI 3.6 shared platform database), the migration documentation needs a follow-up update; that future change is out of scope here.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","