{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b704baa52d753ffd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E003\n3\ttitle: Enable pgvector remote vector_io provider in the OGX distribution\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: OGX Core\n6\tteam: OGX Core\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 9\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Enable pgvector Remote vector_io Provider in the OGX Distribution\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic makes the pgvector remote vector_io provider available and configurable in the OGX distribution so that Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows can use a PostgreSQL/pgvector database running as a separate process instead of the inline Milvus provider. The existing remote vector store implementation in the OGX framework is largely reusable — per the engineering team, the remote pgvector provider already exists in the distribution's provider set — so this epic is configuration and verification work, not new provider development.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Ensure the remote pgvector vector_io provider (`remote::pgvector`, activated via the `ENABLE_PGVECTOR` environment guard) is correctly exposed in the OGX distribution configuration alongside the other remote providers, following the existing provider env-guard pattern used for Milvus and Qdrant.\n38\t- Verify the provider's configuration surface (connection settings, TLS options) accepts the values that a separately provisioned PostgreSQL instance provides, and that it works against the Red Hat `postgresql-16` image (which ships pgvector out of the box — no custom image build and no Konflux onboarding is needed).\n39\t- Confirm the provider runs as a separate process from the OGX server: the vector store is a remote PostgreSQL endpoint, not an inline provider embedded in the OGX process.\n40\t- Verify that OGX's application storage backend is untouched: `storage.kv.type` / `storage.sql.type` remain on their existing SQLite default. This epic changes only the vector_io provider configuration; there is no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL application-storage migration and no consolidation of vector and application storage.\n41\t- Add or extend tests covering the pgvector provider activation and basic vector insert/query behavior.\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- [ ] An OGX server started with the pgvector provider enabled and a reachable PostgreSQL endpoint with pgvector registers the remote pgvector vector_io provider and serves vector insert/query operations through the vector I/O API.\n46\t- [ ] The pgvector provider is activated by configuration only (environment guard), with no code changes required to switch from the inline provider, and the distribution image is unchanged apart from configuration.\n47\t- [ ] The provider operates against a remote PostgreSQL instance running as a separate process; no vector store runs inside the OGX process.\n48\t- [ ] The OGX application storage backend remains on its existing default (SQLite) — no application storage migration occurs.\n49\t- [ ] Automated tests cover pgvector provider activation and basic vector operations, and the distribution's build/test pipeline is green.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability\n52\t\n53\t- HLR-4 (AC-4): The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not an inline provider).\n54\t- HLR-2 (AC-2, partial): Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store — this epic provides the provider capability the playground wiring consumes.\n55\t\n56\t## Risks / Notes\n57\t\n58\t- Per the staff engineer, the remote vector store implementation is largely reusable; if reuse uncovers gaps in the pgvector provider, the additional integration work stays within this epic's scope (provider enablement and verification) and does not change the epic structure.\n59\t- No repo-onboarding, konflux-onboarding, or license-validation chain applies: the distribution repo and build pipeline already exist, and the PostgreSQL image with pgvector is an existing Red Hat image already referenced in the rhods-operator CSV.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98fb192425e37357","agent_id":"a7878a1fe5056a3ff","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"a8b6c0f8-66ba-4092-9b30-e7628b139ec4","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:26:12.286Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b704baa52d753ffd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
