{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82a815cbe5906401","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E006\n3\ttitle: Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: A\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: If RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5 and the\n17\t    go/no-go selects the fallback, remove this branch in favor of BFF-managed provisioning;\n18\t    if that is also rejected, the final no-build fallback is requiring users to bring\n19\t    their own PostgreSQL via the OGXServer CR spec connection details.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 0\n25\t  open_questions: 0\n26\t  external_dependency: -1\n27\t  human_process_gates: 0\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 4\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Operator-Based PostgreSQL/pgvector Self-Provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tThis conditional-branch epic implements operator-based self-provisioning of the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio, following the model-registry-operator pattern: the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) manages the PostgreSQL lifecycle (Deployment, Service, storage, credentials) for playgrounds with RAG, and Gen AI Studio consumes a connection string rather than managing the database itself. This branch applies if the go/no-go decision finds that RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for RHOAI 3.5.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Implement PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer, reusing the model-registry-operator's auto-provisioned database pattern: create and reconcile the PostgreSQL instance (from the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image, which includes pgvector) with resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values (the staff-directed sizing baseline), adjusted per the published benchmark recommendation when available, per the adopted vector store scoping model.\n45\t- Manage the database lifecycle: creation when a playground with RAG is configured, credential secret management, and cleanup on deletion, using the operator's reconciliation loop.\n46\t- Wire the image reference for disconnected installs by reusing the `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` environment variable already present in the rhods-operator CSV — no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding, and no new RELATED_IMAGE entry unless the existing variable cannot be shared (in which case add one following the existing pattern).\n47\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC arrives (RHOAI 3.6), Gen AI Studio consumes the shared connection string instead of the self-provisioned instance; keep the connection source replaceable.\n48\t- Add operator tests covering provisioning, reconciliation, and cleanup.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- [ ] A playground with RAG configured in a namespace gets a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance provisioned automatically (no manual user setup), running as a separate process from the OGX server.\n53\t- [ ] The provisioning follows the adopted vector store scoping model and preserves its multi-tenant isolation guarantees.\n54\t- [ ] The PostgreSQL instance uses the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image (pgvector included) with resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's reference values (adjusted per the published benchmark recommendation when available), and the image reference resolves in disconnected installs via the RELATED_IMAGE mechanism.\n55\t- [ ] Database lifecycle is managed by the operator's reconciliation loop: deletion of the playground configuration cleans up the instance; credential secrets are managed by the operator.\n56\t- [ ] The connection consumed by the playground's OGXServer wiring comes from a single replaceable configuration point, swappable to the RHOAI 3.6 shared platform connection string.\n57\t- [ ] Operator tests cover provisioning, reconciliation, and cleanup, and the build pipeline is green.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- HLR-1 (AC-1): A default vector store is available out-of-the-box without users manually provisioning or configuring external infrastructure.\n62\t- HLR-4 (AC-4, partial): The provisioned database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance.\n63\t- HLR-5 (AC-5, partial): Provisioning follows the adopted multi-tenant scoping model.\n64\t- HLR-6 (AC-6, partial): Sizing from the model-registry-operator reference baseline (benchmark-adjusted when available) keeps the instance lightweight.\n65\t\n66\t## Risks / Notes\n67\t\n68\t- This branch exists only if the go/no-go decision selects operator-based provisioning (RHAISTRAT-7164 landing in time for RHOAI 3.5). If that outcome does not hold, this epic is removed in favor of the BFF-managed provisioning branch (or, as a final no-build fallback, requiring users to bring their own PostgreSQL by configuring the OGXServer CR spec with their connection details).\n69\t- This work cannot start before the operator layer from RHAISTRAT-7164 is available; until it delivers, there is no RHOAI-controlled operator where this lifecycle management can live. The upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic.\n70\t- Gate metadata note: the scalar `gated_by` records only the existence-determining go/no-go gate (the provisioning-approach decision). This branch is also gated by the vector store scoping investigation (listed in `dependencies`): if the adopted scoping model is not per-namespace, the provisioning granularity implemented here is rewritten (different instance count, lifecycle boundaries, and credential separation) — a `rewrite` impact on this epic's scope, not a removal.\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98fb192425e37357","agent_id":"a7878a1fe5056a3ff","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"67dcca60-2029-4c54-83d9-be94c415ac12","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:26:20.238Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-82a815cbe5906401","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
