{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1d94a7192090b3e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E006\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: If RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for RHOAI 3.5 and the go/no-go\n17\t    selects operator-based provisioning, remove this branch in favor of the operator-based\n18\t    branch; if BFF-managed provisioning is also rejected, the final no-build fallback\n19\t    is requiring users to bring their own PostgreSQL via the OGXServer CR spec connection\n20\t    details.\n21\tai_signals:\n22\t  change_specificity: 1\n23\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n24\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n25\t  existing_foundation: 1\n26\t  open_questions: -1\n27\t  external_dependency: 1\n28\t  human_process_gates: 1\n29\t  repo_access: 1\n30\t  architecture_claims: 1\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: High\n34\tai_implementability_score: 7\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\t---\n37\t# BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tThis conditional-branch epic implements BFF-managed provisioning of the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance for Gen AI Studio, as the fallback if the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5. The gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, following the pattern the BFF already uses to provision guardrails infrastructure.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- Implement BFF-managed creation of the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (from the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image, which includes pgvector) with resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values (the staff-directed sizing baseline), adjusted per the published benchmark recommendation when available, per the adopted vector store scoping model, when a playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists in the namespace.\n46\t- Design the lifecycle handling explicitly, since the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, recovery after BFF pod restarts (reconciling expected vs. existing resources on demand), and credential secret management.\n47\t- Wire the image reference for disconnected installs by reusing the `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` environment variable already present in the rhods-operator CSV — no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding, and no new RELATED_IMAGE entry unless the existing variable cannot be shared.\n48\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC arrives (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string instead of self-provisioning; keep the connection source replaceable so the provisioning code path can be retired without restructuring the OGXServer wiring.\n49\t- Add BFF tests covering provisioning, idempotent re-creation checks, cleanup on deletion, and restart recovery.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- [ ] A playground with RAG configured in a namespace gets a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance provisioned automatically by the BFF (no manual user setup), running as a separate process from the OGX server.\n54\t- [ ] The provisioning follows the adopted vector store scoping model and preserves its multi-tenant isolation guarantees.\n55\t- [ ] The PostgreSQL instance uses the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image (pgvector included) with resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's reference values (adjusted per the published benchmark recommendation when available), and the image reference resolves in disconnected installs via the RELATED_IMAGE mechanism.\n56\t- [ ] Lifecycle behavior is implemented and tested: playground deletion cleans up the instance; BFF pod restarts do not orphan or duplicate instances; credentials are managed via secrets.\n57\t- [ ] The connection consumed by the playground's OGXServer wiring comes from a single replaceable configuration point, swappable to the RHOAI 3.6 shared platform connection string.\n58\t- [ ] BFF tests cover provisioning, idempotency, cleanup, and restart recovery, and the build pipeline is green.\n59\t\n60\t## HLR Traceability\n61\t\n62\t- HLR-1 (AC-1): A default vector store is available out-of-the-box without users manually provisioning or configuring external infrastructure.\n63\t- HLR-4 (AC-4, partial): The provisioned database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance.\n64\t- HLR-5 (AC-5, partial): Provisioning follows the adopted multi-tenant scoping model.\n65\t- HLR-6 (AC-6, partial): Sizing from the model-registry-operator reference baseline (benchmark-adjusted when available) keeps the instance lightweight.\n66\t\n67\t## Risks / Notes\n68\t\n69\t- This branch exists only if the go/no-go decision selects the BFF-managed fallback (RHAISTRAT-7164 not landing in time for RHOAI 3.5). If that outcome does not hold, this epic is removed in favor of the operator-based provisioning branch. If BFF-managed provisioning is also rejected, the final no-build fallback is requiring users to bring their own PostgreSQL by manually configuring the OGXServer CR spec with their connection details — consistent with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0, though the Playground largely abstracts OGX from users.\n70\t- The BFF is not an operator: cleanup on deletion, pod-restart recovery, and credential rotation need the explicit design attention called out in the scope; these are the main technical risks of this branch.\n71\t- Gate metadata note: the scalar `gated_by` records only the existence-determining go/no-go gate (the provisioning-approach decision). This branch is also gated by the vector store scoping investigation (listed in `dependencies`): if the adopted scoping model is not per-namespace, the provisioning granularity implemented here is rewritten (different instance count, lifecycle boundaries, and credential separation) — a `rewrite` impact on this epic's scope, not a removal.\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98fb192425e37357","agent_id":"a7878a1fe5056a3ff","session_id":"d39aef87-060e-46a9-9cc3-49e77e4ae642","uuid":"a79f78f6-023f-4e25-ad5c-ec1fe0c7b72a","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:26:20.871Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1d94a7192090b3e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
